--- body ---
## Context
R35 entered the sheet on 2026-08-31 with an `inferred` standing row, which is
the weakest evidence class: neither measured nor read at source. The promise is
that a new, changed or deleted item becomes visible to the index inside the
discovery bound. Two things are missing and they are different jobs.

What upstream does. Incremental updates ride a library version cursor and
deletion reconciles against the key set, so the machinery exists. Nobody here
has established what *triggers* a run, or how often — whether the update path
is a cadence, a hook on a Zotero event, or something that only happens when a
build or a query asks. Until that is read at source the row cannot leave
`inferred`, and R35's standing is a guess in either direction.

What the bound is. SPEC.md §5.2.4 states a reconcile cadence and a disclosed
deletion latency, and R35 delegates its value there on R6's pattern. Whether
those two numbers are the right promise to a user is not settled: they were
chosen as design, before any requirement pointed at them.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] the upstream trigger and cadence read at source at the reviewed SHA, with
      the file and line recorded here
- [ ] deletion latency and new-item latency measured on the reference machine,
      artifact committed under `bench/results/`
- [ ] R35's row moves off `inferred`, or says why it cannot
- [ ] the §2.4 values are confirmed as the bound or re-pinned from the
      measurement, with the ruling in DECISIONS.md if they move

## Relevant files
- `SPEC.md` §3 — R35, and R3 beside it
- `SPEC.md` §5.2.4 — the reconcile cadence and the deletion latency
- `README.md` — R35's standing row
